upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about railway gauges, per Goh Cheng Leong:\n1. The standard gauge (143 cm) is common in Europe, North America and China, and carries the world's fastest, most efficiently run railways.\n2. The broad gauge is more suited to level ground, while the narrow gauge is more likely used across hilly tracts with sharp bends.\n3. Australia's railways were historically built on a single uniform gauge from the outset, since all states coordinated construction from the beginning.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 3 is incorrect and reverses the actual history — the text explicitly describes Australia's railways as built on THREE different gauges (standard in New South Wales, narrow in Queensland/Western Australia, broad in Victoria) independently by each state, causing major inconvenience after federation, not a single coordinated gauge from the start. Statements 1 and 2 are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"Assertion (A): Railways are considered the only convenient form of long-distance transport in large countries and continental interiors such as Siberia.\nReason (R): Trans-continental railways like the Trans-Siberian Railway (about 9,000 km) and Canadian Pacific Railway (about 4,800 km) played a pioneering role in opening up sparsely developed regions like the Russian Steppes/Siberia and the Canadian Prairies.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above Assertion (A) and Reason (R)?","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true and R explains A — Goh Cheng Leong explicitly links the indispensability of railways in vast continental interiors to the historical pioneering role of trans-continental lines like the Trans-Siberian and Canadian Pacific Railways in opening up otherwise inaccessible regions."})

