upsc.append({"q":"Match List-I (Ocean Vessel Type) with List-II (Defining Feature, per Goh Cheng Leong) and select the correct answer using the code below.\nList-I:\nA. Passenger liner\nB. Tramp\nC. Industrial carrier\nD. Coastal shipping vessel\nList-II:\n1. Independent cargo-boat with no fixed schedule, sailing wherever cargo is available\n2. Specially constructed for a single cargo type, e.g. oil tankers or colliers\n3. 'Floating city' on a scheduled route, carrying mainly passengers, mail and limited high-value freight\n4. Plies between coastal harbours and river ports, carrying bulky goods over short local distances\nCodes:","o":["A-3, B-1, C-2, D-4","A-1, B-3, C-2, D-4","A-3, B-2, C-1, D-4","A-3, B-1, C-4, D-2"],"a":0,"e":"The correct match is A-3, B-1, C-2, D-4: passenger liners are scheduled 'floating cities'; tramps are unscheduled independent cargo-boats; industrial carriers (tankers, colliers) are built for a single cargo type; and coastal shipping vessels serve short local routes between coastal harbours."})

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about world shipping routes, per Goh Cheng Leong:\n1. The North Atlantic route is the busiest, linking Western Europe and eastern North America, with about half the world's major ports lying within its sphere.\n2. The Panama Canal route, opened in 1914, mostly handles domestic U.S.A. east-west coast traffic, alongside trade with Caribbean and west-coast South American countries.\n3. The Suez Canal route retained its full former strategic and economic importance even after its 1967 closure and later reopening, unaffected by the growth in oil tanker sizes.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 3 is incorrect — the text explicitly states the Suez Canal, despite reopening, could NEVER regain its former strategic/economic importance, partly due to Middle East political insecurity and partly because it could not accommodate the larger oil tankers (up to 300,000 tonnes) now in use. Statements 1 and 2 are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"According to Goh Cheng Leong's classification of world shipping routes into 'past', 'present' and 'future', which one of the following pairings is correct?","o":["The Suez route as the route of the past; the North/South Atlantic and Cape routes as routes of the present; the Trans-Pacific and Panama routes as routes of the future","The Trans-Pacific route as the route of the past; the Suez route as the route of the present; the Cape route as the route of the future","The Cape route as the route of the past; the Trans-Pacific route as the route of the present; the Suez route as the route of the future","The Panama route as the route of the past; the Trans-Pacific route as the route of the present; the North Atlantic route as the route of the future"],"a":0,"e":"The text explicitly summarises: the Suez route as the route of the past, the North and South Atlantic routes plus the Cape route as routes of the present (the peak of modern trade), and the Trans-Pacific and Panama routes as routes of the future — not any of the other groupings."})

