upsc.append({"q":"Which one of the following statements about Indian Railways, per NCERT, is NOT correct?","o":["India's first railway line, opened in 1853, ran from Bombay to Thane, a distance of only 34 km — the metre gauge is India's most extensive gauge, accounting for over 74% of route length","Indian Railways operates on three gauges: broad (1.676 m), metre (1 m) and narrow (0.762 m or 0.610 m, mostly in hilly areas)","The Konkan Railway (completed 1998), 760 km connecting Roha to Mangalore, crosses 146 rivers/streams, nearly 2,000 bridges and 91 tunnels, including one of Asia's largest rail tunnels","Railway density remains lowest in the hill states, north-eastern states, central India and Rajasthan"],"a":0,"e":"This statement is incorrect — NCERT states the BROAD gauge (not metre gauge) is India's most extensive, accounting for 74.14% of route length, while metre gauge accounts for only about 21%. The other three statements are correct."})

